  "Senza Luna e al transito servirebbero {h}. Sub scelti filtro per filtro: coprono il rumore di lettura con il cielo di stanotte (Luna compresa) e restano nei valori pratici di quel tipo di filtro.": "Without the Moon and at transit it would take {h}. Subs are chosen filter by filter: they swamp the read noise with tonight's sky (Moon included) and stay within the practical range of that kind of filter.",
  "Con i filtri di questo profilo non c’è una strategia adatta a {t}.": "No suitable strategy for {t} with this profile's filters.",
  "Consigli": "Advice", "Campo inquadrato": "Framed field", "nord in alto, est a sinistra": "north up, east left", "Anteprima del campo": "Field preview",
  "Lato lungo a PA": "Long side at PA", "Allinea": "Align", "Mosaico": "Mosaic", "Foto reale": "Real image",
  "Rettangolo, scala e orientamento sono calcolati sul tuo sensore.": "Rectangle, scale and orientation are computed for your sensor.",
  "La notte": "The night", "zona rossa: coperto dall’orizzonte o sotto {a}°": "red area: blocked by the horizon or below {a}°",
  "Passa sopra i grafici per leggere ora e altezza; clicca o trascina per spostare l’ora (si muove anche la cupola).": "Hover the charts to read time and altitude; click or drag to move the time (the dome follows).",
  "Nei prossimi 12 mesi": "Next 12 months", "ore libere col buio, senza contare la Luna · clicca un mese per aprirne la notte più buia": "clear hours in darkness, Moon not counted · click a month to open its darkest night",
  "Come vengono stimati i tempi": "How the times are estimated",
  "HOW1": "Every filter is modelled with its real passbands (manufacturers' data sheets). For each band Skyframe computes how much of the object's light gets through (continuum plus the Hα, [NII], Hβ, OIII and SII lines, weighted by the response of the red, green and blue pixels on a colour camera) and how much sky background: your SQM split into an LED-like continuum and mercury and sodium lines, plus the moonlight of every 5 minutes. The target is followed along its path on the chosen night: at every step its altitude, extinction and the sky in its direction change. The time shown is the one <b>without the Moon</b> along that path, i.e. what the target needs under that sky, comparable across locations and filters; what it would cost with tonight's Moon is shown separately, and the calendar of nights puts in the Moon of each night (or counts only the hours without the Moon, if you choose so in the profile).",
  "HOW2": "The sky background depends on direction: it comes from the lightpollutionmap all-sky map of your spot (or from the Lorenz 2025 atlas), brighter towards the horizon and towards the lights, and changes as the target moves through the night.",
  "HOW3": "Quality is a signal-to-noise ratio per resolution element. The element scales with the aperture (2.3″ at 200 mm, 4.7″ at 100 mm, or the pixel if larger): each telescope is judged at the detail it can deliver, and indeed in real photos the hours hardly depend on the aperture. It is required at three levels: the body of the object (for emission nebulae from the Hα measured in the NSNS and SHASSA surveys, not from the catalogue magnitude), the faint parts (haloes, outer arms) and, when present, the extended dust around it. Bright and small objects are asked to be cleaner, faint and large ones may stay noisier, as imagers do. The “deep” time adds the diffuse Hα measured around the object (Finkbeiner 2003). Weak lines (OIII and SII in an emission nebula) are required in proportion to their strength; for Wolf-Rayet bubbles the outer OIII shell counts too. When two filters pass the same line the signal adds up and the hours are split between them.",
  "HOW4": "Calibration on real photos: 190 public images on AstroBin of 23 objects (nebulae, planetaries, supernova remnants, galaxies, dust), each with its telescope, filters, sky and integration stated. For each one Skyframe redoes the maths with that equipment and that sky and compares with the real hours. “Good” is the median of those photos; “quick” the lower quartile, typical from a city with a colour camera; “excellent” the upper one, typical from a dark site or in mono. On an object left out of the calibration the typical error is a factor of 2.7, and the same photo taken by different people already varies by a factor of 3. With a 200/800 and a colour camera under SQM 19.3: Cocoon 93 h with a quad-band, WR 134 60 h in SHO, NGC 281 8 h in HOO; sky and filters act by plain physics (from SQM 21.3 the Cocoon drops to 16 h). These are estimates to choose, not promises: seeing, transparency and processing matter a lot.",
  "centro inquadratura": "framing centre", "PA lato lungo": "long side PA",
  "Da qui non ha ore utili in nessun periodo dell’anno: col buio non supera {a}° o resta dietro l’orizzonte.": "From here it has no usable hours at any time of year: in darkness it never gets above {a}° or stays behind the horizon.",
  "<b>È il suo periodo.</b> Stanotte {now} libere col buio (il massimo dell’anno è {max}); resta così fino a {to}.": "<b>It's in season.</b> Tonight {now} clear in darkness (the yearly best is {max}); it stays like this until {to}.",
